{"type": "survey_submit", "survey_type": "post", "puzzle_idx": null, "answers": {"puzzle_1_rate_again": 4, "puzzle_1_guesses": "few", "puzzle_2_rate_again": 3, "puzzle_2_guesses": "few", "puzzle_3_rate_again": 4, "puzzle_3_guesses": "few", "puzzle_1_rating_reason": "This puzzle was difficult because I didn't know where to start. The tutorial puzzle and puzzle 2 had very obvious places to start (i.e. one row that could be entirely or nearly deduced right away). This made it easier to deduce the following rows. ", "puzzle_2_rating_reason": "I rated this puzzle a bit easier than the others because I was able to deduce a row from the beginning. From there, I was able to quickly find connections and deduce many other rows. ", "strategy": "For puzzles 2 and 3, I made heavy use of marking off white space. This made the puzzle easier as I didn't need to keep everything in my head. \n\nMy main strategy when solving the puzzles was to find the row/column with the largest number of blacked-out boxes then immediately fill in as much as possible. From there, I would work out from where I started to use the initial deduction.\n\nNot sure if this is what you are looking for in terms of strategy.", "puzzle_3_rating_reason": "I rated this puzzle the same difficulty as puzzle 1 because it was hard to  get started. Mainly because there were many rows/columns that barely needed to be filled in, and so I had a hard time getting going (even if I could roughly deduce the first row).", "comments": "As stated above, a concrete starting area, as well as the amount that needed to be filled in per row/column (since not having much to fill in made the space of possible solutions to consider much larger)."}, "t": "2026-03-30T20:00:11.046287+00:00"}
